screens.body:
Every slide needs a real title placeholder - not a text box that merely looks like a title. Screen readers use the title placeholder to announce which slide you're on, and let someone jump straight between slides; a text box with bold, larger text looks identical on screen but is invisible to that navigation.

**Do:**

- Use a slide layout that includes a Title placeholder and type the slide's own title directly into it.
- {{answer:ppt-q1}}Give every single slide a unique, descriptive title - a screen reader announces an untitled slide only by its number, with nothing to tell you what's actually on it.{{/answer}}
- {{answer:ppt-q23}}Never reuse the exact same title text on more than one slide - PowerPoint's own **Check Accessibility** tool flags a repeated title as a violation, since AT navigation depends on titles actually being unique. For a run of slides covering the same topic, keep the shared title text and add a distinguishing suffix like "(1 of 3)", "(2 of 3)", "(3 of 3)" so each one stays unique while still showing they're related.{{/answer}}
- If a title would take up space you don't have (a slide that's a direct continuation of the one before it, for example), you can move the title placeholder off the edge of the slide instead of deleting it - it stays there for AT to announce, without taking up visible room.
- {{answer:ppt-q25}}To quickly check every slide's title at once, switch to **View > Outline** - it lists only the actual title (and body) placeholder text for the whole deck as one text-only list, so a missing title shows up as a blank line, and a text box that only *looks* like a title (never actually placed in the Title placeholder) won't appear there at all.{{/answer}}
- Run **Review > Check Accessibility** (covered in a later screen) to confirm every slide in the deck actually has one.

**Don't:** Recreate a title's look with a plain text box, font size, and bold - it won't be read as the slide's title no matter how it looks.

If a slide's proofing language is set incorrectly, a screen reader applies the wrong pronunciation rules to everything on it - the words may exist, but they'll be unintelligible.

**Do:** {{answer:ppt-q15}}Confirm the correct language is set under the **Review** tab's language settings - if the deck contains passages in more than one language, set each one correctly so a screen reader can switch pronunciation rules to match.{{/answer}}

**Don't:** Leave the default language setting unchecked and assume it's correct, especially on a deck that started as a copy of one built for a different audience or language.

PowerPoint has a built-in **Accessibility Checker** under the **Review** tab on every platform. It catches a lot of what's covered in this module automatically - missing alt text, low contrast, missing slide titles, and reading order issues.

{{answer:ppt-q20}}On Windows, the checker runs continuously in the background as you work; on Mac, it only runs when you open it manually from the Review tab.{{/answer}}

It's a powerful assistant, but not a substitute for your own judgment - it can flag that alt text is *missing*, but it can't tell you whether the alt text you wrote is actually meaningful, or whether you've relied on color alone somewhere. Use it as a first pass, then read back through your own deck with the concepts from this module in mind.
